0	118	Monika Wulf-Mathies (born 17 March 1942) is a German politician, who was European Commissioner for Regional Policy.



118	247	Early life
Wulf-Mathies was born in the rural town of Wernigerode in 1942 after her family was evacuated from wartime Hamburg.



247	358	Career
In 1971, at the age of 29, Wulf-Mathies joined the then German Chancellor Willy Brandt’s office in Bonn.
359	477	In the government of Chancellor Helmut Schmidt, she later led the Federal Chancellery’s department of social affairs.

477	593	In 1976, Wulf-Mathies left the chancellor’s office to join the managing board of the public service trade union ÖTV.
594	760	Four years later, and to many observers’ surprise, she was elected as the first woman ever to lead one of Germany's most powerful unions, succeeding Heinz Kluncker.  
760	868	In this capacity, she also served as president of Public Services International (PSI) from 1989 until 1995.

868	1033	In 1994, Wulf-Mathies was – again unexpectedly – appointed as one of Germany's two European Commissioners by Chancellor Helmut Kohl; she succeeded Peter Schmidhuber.
1034	1163	From 1994 until 1998, she served as European Commissioner for Regional Policy in the administration of President Jacques Santer.

